direction: down

title: |md
  ## struct net — one per netns
| {near: top-center; shape: text}

netns: "struct net (include/net/net_namespace.h:62)" {
  style.fill: "#dbeafe"
  n_body: |md
    Each network namespace is one of these.
    Holds **everything** that's per-namespace:

    - dev_base_head      — list of net_devices
    - rtnl               — netlink socket for this ns
    - ipv4.fib_main      — IPv4 routing table
    - ipv4.tcp_death_row — TCP timewait state
    - ipv4.fib_notifier_ops
    - ipv6.fib6_main_tbl
    - nft_state          — nftables rules
    - ct.count, ct.htable — conntrack
    - sysctls            — per-ns sysctl table
    - proc_net           — /proc/net for this ns
    - ns                 — common nsfs handle
  |
}

usage: "Per-call dispatch" {
  style.fill: "#fef3c7"
  u_body: |md
    Most net functions take `struct net *net` first.

    Examples:
    - ip_rcv() does `dev_net(dev)` to get the dev's ns.
    - fib_lookup(net, &fl, &res) — different
      result per ns even with same flow.
    - nf_hook(NFPROTO, hook, net, ...) — chains
      registered per-ns.

    Net is a first-class parameter, not a global.
  |
}

shared: "What's NOT per-ns" {
  style.fill: "#fee2e2"
  s_body: |md
    - Hardware (NICs themselves)
    - Slab caches (skbuff_head_cache shared)
    - Route cache slabs
    - Most BPF maps (unless explicitly per-ns)
    - kernel timers
    - process scheduler

    A NIC starts in init_net; can be moved to
    another ns with `ip link set <if> netns <ns>`.
  |
}

netns -> usage
netns -> shared
